From 3b7e81cd329e43611d83fc30752e94a90b55b227 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Filip=20Lewi=C5=84ski?= <filip.lewinski@3mdeb.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 15:23:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] soc/intel/common/block/gpio: keep locking pads outside of
 SMM
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Origin: Dasharo/coreboot `dasharo` branch commit d0635e80c9c870de45e164891f896cb75dd97919 (2026-09-08, direct push)
Upstream-Status: Pending — Gerrit mirror is review.coreboot.org topic tpm_gpio_pltrst_lock (CB:95509-95516; of which 95509/95510/95511 merged to coreboot main as of 2026-10-10); supersedes Dasharo/coreboot PR #959 (closed 2026-09-15, not merged)
Refs: "TPM GPIO fail" https://mkukri.xyz/2024-06-01/tpm-gpio-fail.html ; coreboot ticket #576 ; https://github.com/tlaurion/tpm-gpio-fail

gpio_lock_pad() sent every caller to gpio_lock_pads() as soon as
SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_SMM_LOCK_GPIO_PADS was selected, including the
callers in gpio_configure_pads(). That function runs in ramstage, so
gpio_lock_pads() bailed out with 'must be called from SMM!' and the pads
requesting a lock in the mainboard pad tables, i.e. every PAD_CFG_*_LOCK
entry, were left unlocked.

Route non-SMM callers to gpio_non_smm_lock_pad() regardless of the SMM
locking option, so that both ways of locking a pad can be used on the
same board.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Filip Lewiński <filip.lewinski@3mdeb.com>
---
 src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/gpio.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/gpio.c b/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/gpio.c
index 6f71c19ae4..bd62928edc 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/gpio.c
+++ b/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/gpio.c
@@ -736,7 +736,12 @@ int gpio_lock_pad(const gpio_t pad, enum gpio_lock_action lock_action)
 		.lock_action = lock_action
 	};
 
-	if (!ENV_SMM && !CONFIG(SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_SMM_LOCK_GPIO_PADS))
+	/*
+	 * gpio_lock_pads() only works in SMM. Pads locked while configuring
+	 * them, i.e. from the pad tables in ramstage, have to go through the
+	 * non-SMM path even when the SoC also locks pads from SMM.
+	 */
+	if (!ENV_SMM)
 		return gpio_non_smm_lock_pad(&pads);
 
 	return gpio_lock_pads(&pads, 1);
-- 
